Janis Papanagnou <Janis_Papanagnou@hotmail.com> wrote:

> Is there some rationale lying behind this
> "divisible by three" word sizes?  Could you please
> elaborate, if so, and if you know about it.
> Though I am well aware of CDC's 60 bit, TR440 48
> bit, Burrows 36 bit, I neither thought of any
> underlying principle, nor heard of one, yet.

Partially it hearkens back to the days of COBOL, and
6 bit characters, from the really aarly days of
computing. Six bits gets you an upper case 26 letter
alphabet, the ten digits, and still leaves room for
28 characters of punctuation, which more than
sufficed for early business programming needs. It
just seemed natural to make the word sizes a
multiple of six, and there were _lots_ of computers
with that style of word lengths, from DEC,
Honeywell, Harris, Data General, besides the ones
you mention, and I'm sure lots of others I don't
know. Perhaps octal notation, which encodes three
bits per octal digit exerted some "mindset" pressure
that direction too. Past that, I don't know.

The very first computer I ever used, the IBM 1620,
had (IIUC) a 48 bit word size, but weirdly done, as
12 nibbles of four bits, each used to encode a
decimal digit! Back when each bit was a physical
object you could pick up in your fingers, an
iron-compound ring threaded by three wires, tossing
in a couple of extra bits to make word lengths a
power of two probably looked like sheerest
impractical economic fantasy.

